  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach is a hybrid optimization that combines global random exploration with local gradient-based search. The key bottleneck is its limited exploration at finer scales and potentially suboptimal local optima trapping in periodic components. We propose:\n\n- Swapping between fine and medium resolution levels (128 and 256 points) to escape local optima\n- Introducing a frequency-space analysis component to measure \"periodicity\"\n- Using a smoothing factor in the objective function to manage large gradient jumps\n- Implementing tighter coupling between global and local optimization stages\n\nOur central idea is to use differential evolution at medium resolution for structural optimization, then project to high resolution for detailed optimization. The approach balances exploration (using DE) with exploitation (using gradient-based search).\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe consider h as a step function with fixed integral \u222bh dx = 1 [= n/2 for discrete case]\nThe objective is the maximum of:\n    C5 = \u20bc max \u06a9\u786c\u5316 def only in samples [0, n-1] \n        \nFor each lag d:\n    corr(d) = sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] * (1-h[(i+d) % n])   (for circular structure)\n\nIn the implementation, we use:\n    C5 = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * (2.0/n)\n\nConstraints:\n    0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n    sum(h) == n/2\n\nThe optimization formulation uses continuous variables with box constraints and equality constraint.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization: \n   - Start at parent resolution (n=140 samples) \n   - Generate additional initializations at n=128 (coarser) and n=256 (finer)\n\nb. Outsourcing the optimization to stages:\n   Phase 1 (Global Exploration):\n      - Use differential evolution on a medium resolution grid (n=128)\n      - Minimize a surrogate objective that approximates the circular correlation behavior\n      - Store best candidate\n\n   Phase 2 (Local Refinement):\n      - Project from medium resolution (n=128) to higher resolution (n=256)\n      - Use SLSQP-based local optimization with adaptive step sizes\n      - Combine gradient and penalty methods for constraint handling\n\nc. Dimension management:\n   - Coresolution conversion: use linear interpolation for translating samples\n\nd. Evaluation metrics:\n   - Primary: C5 (as defined in the problem)\n   - Secondary: \"periodicity\" = ratio of top two correlation values\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\na. Default configuration:\n   - Medium resolution: n=128\n   - High resolution: n=256\n   - DE population: 100 individuals\n   - DE F: 0.8, CR: 0.9\n   - SLSQP settings: \n        maxiter=500 initially,\n        ftol=1e-8\n        bounds = [0.0,1.0] for all variables\n\nb. Frequency analysis adapter:\n   - Add frequency-finder that flags periodicity in solution\n   - Periodicity threshold: peaks in autocorrelation above global average\n\nc. Parameter adjustments:\n   - If periodicity flags too high: increase mixing in DE\n   - If convergence is slow: increase DE population and raise SLSQP iterations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\na. Dominant costs:\n   - The differential evolution stage is most expensive\n   - Time allocation:\n        DE: 700s (n=128)\n        Refinement: 400s (n=256)\n\nb. Feasibility checks:\n   - Compute periodicity of candidates \n   - Verify sum of samples = n/2\n   - Skip natureinspired methods for bad periodicity ratios\n\nc. Termination conditions:\n   - Time-based cutoffs before expensive stages\n   - Early termination for stuck candidates\n\n6. Validation and return contract\na. Post-optimization checks:\n   - Evaluate candidate using exact correlation method\n   - Skip surrogate-based evaluation if periodicity analyzer flags periodic structure\n\nb. Output requirements:\n   - Return valid h with 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)=n/2\n   - Include detailed timing statistics and evolution statistics",
